   "answer": "/etc holds system wide configuration files. /var holds data that changes while the system runs, such as logs in /var/log, caches and spools. /home holds the users’ own folders. /usr holds installed programs and libraries, read only in normal use. /tmp is scratch space that may be cleaned at boot. /proc is not on disk at all: the kernel generates it to show processes and kernel settings as files.",
   "explain": "<p>A good follow up is /dev (device files such as /dev/sda) and /boot (the kernel and initramfs). On Ubuntu /bin and /sbin are links into /usr.</p>",
