   "id": "umask",
   "topic": "Files and permissions",
   "lv": "m",
   "q": "The umask is 027. What modes do a new file and a new folder get, and why?",
   "hint": "Start from 666 and 777.",
   "answer": "Programs create files with 666 and folders with 777 as the starting mode, and the kernel removes the bits set in the umask. 027 removes write for the group and everything for others. So a file gets 640 (rw-r-----) and a folder 750 (rwxr-x---).",
   "explain": "<p>The umask removes bits, it does not subtract digits. With umask 033 a file still gets 644, because 666 has no x bits to remove.</p>",
