   "answer": "SUID on a program makes it run with the rights of the file’s owner: /usr/bin/passwd is owned by root and SUID, so a normal user can change their own password in /etc/shadow. SGID on a folder makes new files inside belong to the folder’s group, which suits team folders. The sticky bit on a folder lets only the owner of a file delete or rename it, which is why everyone can write to /tmp (mode 1777) but not delete other people’s files.",
   "explain": "<p>In ls -l they show as s in the owner or group x position and t in the others x position. A capital S or T means the bit is set but x is not. SUID programs are a common way up for attackers, so audit them with find / -perm -4000.</p>",
