   "q": "What is the difference between a hard link and a symbolic link?",
   "hint": "Think of inodes.",
   "answer": "A hard link is a second name for the same inode, so both names are equal and the data stays until the last name is removed. Hard links cannot cross file systems and normally cannot point to folders. A symbolic link is a small file that holds a path. It can point anywhere, also to folders and other file systems, but it breaks (dangles) when the target is moved or deleted.",
   "explain": "<p>ls -li shows the inode number and the link count. rm removes one name and lowers the count. A file’s space is freed when the count is 0 and no process has it open.</p>",
