   "answer": "An inode holds the metadata of a file: type, mode bits, owner and group, size, timestamps (access, modify, change), the link count and where the data blocks are. It does not hold the file name. Names live in folder entries that map a name to an inode number. That is why one inode can have several names (hard links) and why renaming in the same file system is instant.",
   "explain": "<p>File systems such as ext4 have a fixed number of inodes, so a disk can be “full” with free space left when millions of tiny files use them all up. df -i shows inode use.</p>"
