   "lv": "m",
   "q": "How does the shell start a program such as ls?",
   "hint": "Two system calls, and a wait.",
   "answer": "The shell calls fork, which makes a copy of the shell process with a new PID. The child calls execve with /usr/bin/ls, which replaces its program with ls while keeping the PID, the open files and the environment. The parent shell calls wait and gets the exit status when the child ends.",
