   "hint": "Overcommit and badness.",
   "answer": "Linux overcommits memory: it promises more than it has, expecting not all of it to be used. When RAM and swap run out and nothing can be reclaimed, the OOM killer picks the process with the highest badness score (mostly the one using the most memory, adjusted by oom_score_adj) and kills it with SIGKILL. Protect a service with OOMScoreAdjust= (down to -1000) in its unit, and better, put limits such as MemoryMax= on the other services so they are killed inside their own cgroup first.",
